Pre-allocate, do not grow
Same answer, two ways. Watch the clock.

grow &lt;- function(k) {
  out &lt;- c()                                  # grows on every iteration
  for (i in seq_len(k)) out &lt;- c(out, sqrt(i))
  out
}

prealloc &lt;- function(k) {
  out &lt;- numeric(k)                           # the right size, once
  for (i in seq_len(k)) out[i] &lt;- sqrt(i)
  out
}

system.time(grow(50000))["elapsed"]

elapsed 
   5.14 

system.time(prealloc(50000))["elapsed"]

elapsed 
      0

The same job, twice
Task. For every outcome, the treatment-minus-placebo difference in means.



vapply(outcomes, \(v) {
  m &lt;- tapply(trial[[v]],
              trial$arm, mean)
  round(m[["Treatment"]] -
        m[["Placebo"]], 1)
}, numeric(1))

 sbp_0  sbp_6 sbp_12 
  -1.5   -9.4  -12.4 



map_dbl(outcomes, \(v) {
  trial |&gt;
    group_by(arm) |&gt;
    summarise(m = mean(.data[[v]])) |&gt;
    pull(m) |&gt;
    diff() |&gt;
    round(1)
}) |&gt; set_names(outcomes)



Same numbers. vapply() names its result for free; map_dbl() needs set_names(). And v holds a string, so it is .data[[v]] — not last week’s curly-curly, which wants a bare column name.

if, else if, else
if takes one TRUE or FALSE and picks a branch.

n_missing &lt;- sum(is.na(trial_raw$sbp_12))

if (n_missing == 0) {
  "complete outcome data"
} else if (n_missing &lt; 5) {
  "a few missing — a complete-case summary is defensible"
} else {
  "too much missing outcome data to summarise naively"
}

[1] "a few missing &lt;U+2014&gt; a complete-case summary is defensible"





The condition is a single logical value. Here, n_missing == 0.
else must sit on the same line as the closing }, or R decides the if has ended.
Braces are optional for one-line bodies. Use them anyway.

A function is three things

sbp_summary &lt;- function(x, digits = 1) {
  round(c(n = length(x), mean = mean(x), sd = sd(x)), digits)
}

formals(sbp_summary)
body(sbp_summary)
environment(sbp_summary)

$x


