Plausible code can answer the wrong question
visit |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(week == 4, !is.na(marker)) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    n = dplyr::n(),
    mean_marker = mean(marker),
    sd_marker = sd(marker),
    .by = arm
  )
The code runs, but n is observed n, not total n, because missing rows were removed first.

Other common failures

joining without checking duplicate keys;
silently removing missing observations;
using an unexpected denominator;
inventing a function or argument;
changing a working style more broadly than requested;
producing a plot whose labels do not match its calculation.

Good first uses are small and checkable

complete a familiar function call;
draft repetitive column names or plotting labels;
translate one already-understood base-R operation to dplyr;
propose a test case for an existing function;
explain an error message using synthetic context.

Avoid beginning with a full analysis whose answer you cannot independently describe.

Context creates a privacy boundary
AI completion can use surrounding code and open-file context. Never expose:

protected health information or identifiable records;
restricted or confidential research data;
passwords, tokens, private keys, or .Renviron;
unpublished confidential material not approved for the service.

Use only synthetic course data in this session.

Numbering, looking forward, and baselines

sbp_long |&gt;
  arrange(id, week) |&gt;
  group_by(id) |&gt;
  mutate(
    visit      = row_number(),
    next_sbp   = lead(sbp),
    from_base  = sbp - first(sbp)
  ) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  head(6) |&gt;
  as.data.frame()

    id     arm week sbp visit next_sbp from_base
1 P001 Placebo    0 160     1      146         0
2 P001 Placebo    6 146     2      144       -14
3 P001 Placebo   12 144     3       NA       -16
4 P002 Placebo    0 162     1      165         0
5 P002 Placebo    6 165     2      156         3
6 P002 Placebo   12 156     3       NA        -6



row_number() numbers rows within group · lead() looks forward · first() takes the group’s first value · cumsum() accumulates.

Exit evidence
Show the instructor or TA:

an empty RStudio Git pane;
History containing the three core commits;
the line-by-line diff for one chosen commit;
a successful Push result; and
git remote -v showing private origin and instructor upstream;
the browser address of the private BIOS6301-mywork repository; and
Settings → Collaborators showing invitations for the instructor and TA.

Explain the difference between a saved edit, a staged change, a local commit, and a commit pushed to GitHub.

Software
The primary course environment consists of:

R;
RStudio during the foundational portion of the course;
Quarto for reproducible reports and presentations;
Git and GitHub for version control and submission workflow;
selected tidyverse packages;
Positron beginning in Session 9;
GitHub Copilot beginning in Session 9 under the policy below; and
Python, NumPy, pandas, and notebook-based computing for the introductory Python session.

Course examples use synthetic or unrestricted teaching data. Students must not enter protected, identifiable, or restricted data into course repositories or public AI services.

Base R and tidyverse policy
The course treats base R and tidyverse as two useful approaches within the same R language. Students are not required to adopt one dialect exclusively.
Unless an exercise explicitly targets a particular method, students may choose the approach that makes the analysis easiest to read and verify. Evaluation will focus on:

correctness of the result;
clarity of the data and statistical meaning;
explicit handling of missing values and edge cases;
validation of important assumptions and intermediate results; and
reproducibility and readability.

Students should nevertheless learn to read both styles and occasionally verify an important result using a second approach.

Review the diff as a statistical reviewer
For an analysis change, verify:

the intended rows changed;
the cohort or inclusion rule did not change accidentally;
missing-value handling remains explicit;
no credential or private path appeared;
the interpretation matches the calculation; and
the document still renders.

“The file changed” is less useful than “these lines changed for this reason.”

Security is part of the workflow
Never commit:

passwords, access tokens, or private keys;
.Renviron containing secrets;
protected health information;
restricted research data; or
identifiable participant-level teaching examples.

If sensitive information is committed, deleting the visible line is not enough. Contact the instructor or appropriate security support immediately.

